## 1. Problem Statement
Basic calculators can only process one operation at a time, evaluate expressions in an unsafe way using `eval()`, provide unclear error messages and lack a calculation history.
We need a safer, more reliable calculator that validates its input, provides informative error messages and provides historical information.

## 2. Objectives
1. Overcome the limitations of a standard calculator, including the unsafe nature of `eval()`, lack of informative error messages and lack of history.
2. Design a modular and layered system, including modules for arithmetic operations, expression evaluation, and history.
3. Build the calculator in Python using functions, modules, and exception handling, file access and the `ast` module.
4. Ensure code reliability and security through validation, custom exception types, limiting resources and logging.
5. Ensure code correctness with automated testing of normal, edge, and error cases.
6. Document the code with requirements, UML diagrams, a README file, and a project report.

## 3. Functional Requirements

### Module 1 - Arithmetic Operations
| ID | Description | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-1 | Add, subtract, multiply, divide two numbers | Menu choice, 2 numbers | Numeric result |
| FR-2 | Power and modulus of two numbers | Menu choice, 2 numbers | Numeric result |
| FR-3 | Square root of one number | Menu choice, 1 number | Numeric result |
| FR-4 | Reject division/modulus by zero and negative square roots | Invalid operands | Clear error message |

### Module 2 - Expression Evaluator
| ID | Description | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-5 | Evaluate expressions with `+ - / %`, brackets and unary signs | Text, e.g. `2 + 3 (4 - 1)` | Numeric result |
| FR-6 | Reject unsafe or malformed expressions | Text such as `__import__('os')` | Error message |

### Module 3 - History & Reports
| ID | Description | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| FR-7 | Record every calculation with timestamp | Expression, result | Entry in `history.json` |
| FR-8 | View history and summary statistics (count, average, min, max) | Menu choice | Console listing |
| FR-9 | Export history to CSV | Filename | `.csv` file |
| FR-10 | Clear history | Menu choice | Empty history |

### User Workflow
1. Launch the application and view the main menu.
2. Choose Basic Operation, Expression Evaluator or History & Reports.
3. Enter the requested input, which is validated.
4. The result (or a clear error) is displayed and stored in the history.
5. Successful calculations are saved to history, the user is returned to the menu or exits.

## 4. Non-Functional Requirements
| ID | Category | Description | How it is met |
|---|---|---|---|
| NFR-1 | Security | Never execute arbitrary code from user input | `ast`-based evaluator allows only numbers and arithmetic operators |
| NFR-2 | Reliability | Application must not crash on bad input or a corrupt data file | Custom exceptions caught in `cli.py`; `HistoryManager.load()` recovers from bad JSON |
| NFR-3 | Usability | Clear menus and readable error messages | Numbered menus; integers shown without `.0` |
| NFR-4 | Performance | Each calculation completes in under 1 second | Constant-time operations; exponent capped at 1000 |
| NFR-5 | Resource efficiency | Bounded memory and file size | History capped at 100 entries; expression length capped at 200 characters |
| NFR-6 | Maintainability | Easy to extend and test | Single-purpose modules, operation registry, docstrings, config constants |
| NFR-7 | Logging | Operations and errors are traceable | File logging to `logs/calculator.log` |
| NFR-8 | Scalability | New operations can be added without changing existing code | Operation registry (`OPERATIONS` dict) in `operations.py`; modules are loosely coupled |
| NFR-9 | Error handling strategy | All expected failures are reported clearly and never crash the app | Custom exception hierarchy rooted at `CalculatorError`, caught centrally in `cli.py` and logged |

